Team Roles & Responsibilities (Python 3 Stack Shift)
Overview

6 team members, each with a clear focus on building our procedural map generation engine in pure Python 3. Each person owns one isolated piece with standard interfaces.

Role 1: Project Coordinator & DevOps Lead

What they own: Project structure, Git workflow, Docker containerization, CI/CD automated checks.
What they do:
1. Maintain repo folder structure and python package organization (`/backend`, `/frontend`, `/docs`).
2. Set up GitHub Actions CI workflow to run `flake8` linting and `pytest` test suites on PRs.
3. Configure `Dockerfile` and `docker-compose.yml` for local development (Flask app + Python dev environment).
4. Establish git branching rules (`main` protected, PR review required).
5. Maintain `CONTRIBUTING.md` and setup guides.

Who they work with: Everyone (coordinates workflow and merges).

Role 2: Backend/Server Developer

What they own: Python Flask application server and REST API contracts.
What they do:
1. Build Flask server (`backend/app.py`) listening for client map generation requests.
2. Implement REST endpoints:
   - `POST /api/generate`: accepts algorithm choice, seed, scale, octaves, detail → calls Python algorithm modules → returns tile matrix JSON.
   - `POST /api/export/image`: receives tile data → delegates to Image Export module → returns PNG image stream.
   - `POST /api/export/json`: receives tile data → formats downloadable JSON file.
   - `GET /health`: system health check for DevOps monitoring.
3. Configure CORS headers, global error handlers, and HTTP status codes.
4. Write API unit tests using `pytest` and `FlaskClient`.

Who they work with: Algorithm Dev (calls pure python functions), Export Dev, Frontend Dev.

Role 3: Procedural Generation Developer (Algorithm Core)

What they own: Pure Python map generation algorithms (No Flask imports allowed).
What they do:
1. Write 3 pure Python procedural generation modules:
   - `perlin.py`: Smooth Value & Perlin Noise (multi-octave terrain, continuous height maps).
   - `cellular.py`: Cellular Automata (4-5 rule cave solver with state hysteresis and flood-fill cleanup).
   - `bsp.py`: Binary Space Partitioning (Recursive spatial tree splitting, room carving, corridor path graph).
2. Ensure deterministic seed output (same seed always produces identical grid matrix).
3. Benchmark algorithm performance (ensure generation completes under 5ms for standard 20,000 tile grids).
4. Write unit tests verifying seed reproducibility, boundary limits, and edge cases.

Who they work with: Backend Dev (provides pure functions returning tile matrices).

Role 4: Image & Export Developer

What they own: Data serialization and visual image rendering in Python.
What they do:
1. Use Python Pillow (`PIL`) to convert 2D tile matrices into PNG map images.
2. Build biome palette mapping (Water, Sand, Meadow, Forest, Cave Wall, Dungeon Floor, Corridors).
3. Support variable output resolution exports (1x, 2x, 4x tile scaling).
4. Build JSON serializer for tile matrices.
5. Write `pytest` test suite to verify image headers and JSON validity.

Who they work with: Backend Dev (receives tile data, returns PNG streams or JSON strings).

Role 5: Frontend UI Developer

What they own: Website interface, Python code documentation, interactive canvas, and live sandbox.
What they do:
1. Build modern HTML5 / CSS3 / Vanilla JS single-page web app and dedicated documentation pages.
2. Create dedicated web pages for all algorithms (`perlin.html`, `cellular.html`, `bsp.html`) and data structures (`spatial-grid.html`, `bsp-tree.html`, `moore-neighborhood.html`, `spatial-hash.html`).
3. Document all algorithm implementations in clean, readable Python code blocks.
4. Render real-time map previews on HTML5 Canvas using JS/Fetch API.
5. Position the Interactive Live Sandbox at the end of `index.html` as the primary playground.

Who they work with: Backend Dev (connects UI controls to API endpoints).

Role 6: Quality & Documentation Lead

What they own: End-to-end testing, Python test coverage, and developer/user documentation.
What they do:
1. Write full-pipeline integration tests (`pytest`) covering generate → export → download flow.
2. Measure test coverage (`pytest --cov=backend`).
3. Maintain mathematical guides explaining noise math, cellular automata 4-5 rule hysteresis, and tree partitions.
4. Verify setup guide (`SETUP.md`) and API documentation (`API.md`).

Who they work with: Everyone (coordinates quality gates).

Simplified Workflow
1. Coordinator sets up Python workspace, Docker, and CI workflow.
2. Algorithm Dev writes pure Python procedural functions.
3. Backend Dev wraps functions in Flask API routes.
4. Export Dev adds Pillow PNG rendering & JSON serialization.
5. Frontend Dev updates website with Python docs, dedicated pages, and live sandbox at bottom.
6. QA Lead runs integration tests and verifies test coverage.